# Build context exclusions — BASELINE for every Dockerfile in this repository.
#
# This file is the only exclusion list that applies to BOTH builders:
#
#   * BuildKit additionally honours `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
#     (agent/Dockerfile.dockerignore, cmd/sentinel/Dockerfile.dockerignore), and
#     when one exists it REPLACES this file rather than adding to it.
#   * The classic builder has no such mechanism. It reads this file and nothing
#     else.
#
# That asymmetry is the whole reason this file is a floor and not a policy. It must
# never exclude a path ANY Dockerfile COPYs, or the Sentinel build breaks on the
# classic builder while appearing to work on BuildKit. Per-image leanness belongs
# in the per-Dockerfile files; correctness belongs here.
#
# History, because the failure this shape prevents is easy to repeat.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An earlier version of this file ended with:
#
#     # Nothing outside agent/ is consumed by the image
#     internal/
#     cmd/
#
# That comment is true of agent/Dockerfile and false of
# cmd/sentinel/Dockerfile, which COPYs `cmd/` and `internal/`. The Sentinel build
# therefore failed at `COPY internal/ internal/` with
#
#     failed to calculate checksum ... "/internal": not found
#
# on a repository where the directory plainly existed. Go source is not a
# credential, and excluding it bought nothing; it only made one of the two images
# unbuildable. `.github/workflows/e2e-detonation.yaml` builds the Sentinel from this
# same repository-root context and would fail identically. The claim in the removed
# comment was true only for the image its author happened to be building, which is
# precisely why it read as a general rule.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control and CI definitions
#
# `.git/` is the largest single item in the context and is also the one most likely
# to carry credentials: `.git/config` holds remote URLs that may embed a token.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git/
.github/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials and tool configuration
#
# `.gitignore` protects these from being committed. That is a DIFFERENT channel.
# The build context is uploaded to the builder on every `docker build`, so a file
# that `.gitignore` protects is still shipped unless this file says otherwise.
# The two must be kept in step deliberately, never assumed to agree.
#
# Every pattern below is a credential SHAPE, not a filename of one known asset.
# `opencode.json` and `.mcp.json` are named explicitly because both are known to
# carry `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN`; a first `git add -A` here committed one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
opencode.json
.mcp.json

# Environment files, matched at any depth rather than only at the root. A first
# attempt anchored these with a leading slash so `.env.example` would survive by
# accident; a negative control then showed the cost — a token in `agent/.env` was
# unprotected, which is just as leaked as one at the root. So: broad pattern,
# explicit re-admission of the example forms.
.env*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template

# Cluster credentials. The E2E harness reads KUBECONFIG from the environment, so a
# developer's kubeconfig landing in the repository root is an expected accident.
# On the current host this is a live risk rather than a hypothetical one:
# /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml is root-owned mode 0600 and every kubectl call uses
# sudo, so a `sudo cp` into the worktree is one careless command away.
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.yaml
*.kubeconfig

# Key material. `*.key` is narrow: this repository has no tracked file matching it
# today, which was checked rather than assumed, and a Secret fixture named
# `something.key` would be a manifest test rather than key material.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Cloud and package credentials, which follow developers into a repository root by
# habit rather than by intent.
.aws/
.gcloud/
credentials.json
service-account*.json
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kubernetes Secret manifests
#
# Deliberately narrow. A redacted Secret fixture is a legitimate thing to commit
# and blocking it would be wrong, so this is scoped to the repository root and
# `deploy/**` can still hold reviewed, redacted fixtures for the deploy tests.
# Nothing tracked matches this today.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/secrets.yaml
/secrets.yml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local virtual environments
#
# `.venv*/` rather than `.venv/`, because the interpreter this project's gates
# require is a specific minor version: `agent/pyproject.toml` pins
# `target-version = ["py311"]` and root `setup.cfg` sets `python_version = 3.11`,
# so the environment is named `.venv311` and is load-bearing rather than
# incidental. Uploading it would add roughly 300 MB of site-packages to every
# build context for no benefit.
#
# The cost of the glob is recorded rather than hidden: `.venv*/` also matches a
# hypothetical `.venv311-broken/`. That is the correct trade — an occasional stray
# directory excluded from a build context is harmless, whereas shipping site-
# packages is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv*/
**/.venv*/
venv/
**/venv/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artefacts
#
# `/bin/` is the Sentinel build output (`go build -o bin/sentinel`). Anchored so a
# legitimately named `bin/` inside a subproject is not swept up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/bin/
/.gotmp/
/pprof/
scrubber.test
*.test
coverage.*
*.out
cpu*.out
ci-g3-output.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python build and test caches
#
# `__pycache__` matters specifically: it holds bytecode compiled by whatever
# interpreter the developer happened to use. Copying it into an image built on a
# different Python version ships stale, useless bytecode and inflates the layer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.egg-info/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors, OS and local scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~
tmpdump/
probe_*.go

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT excluded here, deliberately
#
# internal/    cmd/       — cmd/sentinel/Dockerfile COPYs both. Excluding them
#                           breaks the Sentinel build; see the history note above.
# docs/        *.md       — no image COPYs them, but excluding them here would
#                           make this file a per-image policy, which is what the
#                           per-Dockerfile files are for.
# tests/                   — likewise. The per-Dockerfile files drop it; this
#                           baseline keeps the floor safe for any builder.
#
# Kept out of this file so the baseline stays correct no matter which Dockerfile
# is built, and so the reason a path is absent is always recorded next to the
# claim that it should be absent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
